ITEMS = [
("In what order does FortiGate evaluate firewall policies?",
 ["Top to bottom, first match", "Lowest policy ID first", "Most specific match first", "Bottom to top, last match"], "A",
 "Policies are checked from the top of the list down, and the first policy whose criteria fit the traffic is applied. The policy ID is only a label and does not set the order."),
("Traffic matches no firewall policy. What does FortiGate do with it?",
 ["Allows it and logs it", "Drops it by implicit deny", "Sends it to the default route", "Queues it for admin review"], "B",
 "The implicit deny policy (ID 0) at the end of the list drops anything no policy matched. Logging of these drops can be turned on from the implicit deny entry."),
("An administrator moves policy ID 9 above policy ID 3. What changes?",
 ["Policy 9 gets ID 3", "Both IDs are renumbered", "Only the evaluation order", "Nothing until a reboot"], "C",
 "Moving a policy changes its position in the list, which is what FortiGate evaluates; the policy ID stays the same. IDs are fixed identifiers used in logs."),
("Which tool shows which policy would match a given source, destination and service?",
 ["diagnose sniffer packet", "FortiView sessions", "Log and report", "Policy lookup"], "D",
 "Policy lookup simulates a packet and highlights the policy it would match. A sniffer shows packets but not which policy they hit."),
("Several interfaces should share one set of firewall policies. What should the administrator use?",
 ["A zone", "A VDOM link", "An aggregate interface", "A software switch"], "A",
 "A zone groups interfaces so a policy can use the zone as its incoming or outgoing interface. An aggregate bonds links for bandwidth, and a software switch bridges them into one subnet."),
("By default, can traffic pass between two interfaces in the same zone?",
 ["Yes, always allowed", "No, intrazone is denied", "Only for local-in traffic", "Only through a VIP"], "B",
 "Zones are created with intrazone set to deny, so traffic between member interfaces needs a policy or intrazone set to allow."),
("In a firewall policy, an Internet Service Database (ISDB) object replaces which fields?",
 ["Source address only", "Schedule and service", "Destination and service", "Incoming interface"], "C",
 "An Internet Service entry carries both the IP ranges and the ports of the service, so it is used in place of the destination address and service. FortiGuard keeps it updated."),
("A recurring schedule has a start time of 18:00 and an end time of 06:00. How does it behave?",
 ["It is rejected as invalid", "It runs 06:00 to 18:00", "It runs on one day only", "It runs overnight"], "D",
 "When the end time is earlier than the start time, a recurring schedule runs past midnight and ends the next morning."),
("In diagnose sys session list output, which field shows the policy a session matched?",
 ["policy_id", "proto_state", "npu_state", "serial"], "A",
 "policy_id shows which firewall policy accepted the session. proto_state shows the protocol state, and serial is the session's own number."),
("A policy's Log Allowed Traffic is set to All Sessions. What gets logged?",
 ["Only profile-event sessions", "Every session it allows", "Only sessions it denies", "Only long-lived sessions"], "B",
 "All Sessions writes a traffic log for every session the policy accepts, whether or not a profile acts on it. Security Events logs only sessions where a profile raised an event, which uses far less log space."),
("What kind of traffic do local-in policies control?",
 ["Traffic between two VDOMs", "Traffic leaving the FortiGate", "Traffic to the FortiGate itself", "Traffic through an IPsec tunnel"], "C",
 "Local-in policies filter traffic addressed to the FortiGate's own interfaces, such as admin access or routing protocols. Firewall policies handle traffic passing through."),
("An administrator wants to stop GUI access on one interface. What is the simplest way?",
 ["Add a deny firewall policy", "Apply a DoS policy", "Use an IPS sensor", "Clear HTTPS in allowaccess"], "D",
 "The interface allowaccess list controls which admin protocols the FortiGate answers on that interface, so removing HTTPS stops the GUI there. A firewall policy does not control traffic to the FortiGate itself."),
("Policy 1 accepts all sources and services. Policy 2, below it, denies HTTP from 10.0.1.0/24. What happens to HTTP from 10.0.1.5?",
 ["Policy 1 allows it", "Policy 2 denies it", "Implicit deny drops it", "Both policies log it"], "A",
 "The first matching policy wins, and policy 1 matches everything, so policy 2 is never reached. Specific policies belong above general ones."),
("In policy-based NGFW mode, where are applications and URL categories matched?",
 ["In the firewall policy", "In a security policy", "In a proxy policy", "In the SSL profile"], "B",
 "Policy-based mode uses security policies to match applications, URL categories and users. In profile-based mode the same controls are profiles attached to the firewall policy."),
("Which address type matches a host name such as www.example.com?",
 ["Subnet address", "IP range address", "FQDN address", "Geography address"], "C",
 "An FQDN address resolves the name through DNS and keeps its matched IPs current. A geography address matches by country."),
("An administrator wants a policy active only during business hours. What is attached to it?",
 ["A traffic shaper", "A virtual IP", "A service group", "A schedule"], "D",
 "A schedule, one-time or recurring, limits when a policy can match. Outside the schedule the policy is skipped and lower policies are checked."),
("Which shaper type applies one bandwidth limit shared across all traffic it covers?",
 ["Shared shaper", "Per-IP shaper", "Application shaper", "Outbound shaper"], "A",
 "A shared shaper applies its guaranteed and maximum bandwidth to all the traffic it is applied to together. A per-IP shaper applies the limit to each source IP separately."),
("How are IPv4 and IPv6 firewall policies handled in FortiOS 7.6?",
 ["They need separate tables", "One policy can hold both", "IPv6 needs its own VDOM", "IPv6 has no policies"], "B",
 "IPv4 and IPv6 share one consolidated policy table, so a single policy can carry both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses. Separate IPv6 policy tables were removed in FortiOS 6.4."),
("In diagnose sys session list output, which value counts down while a session is idle?",
 ["duration", "packets", "expire", "timeout"], "C",
 "expire shows the seconds left before an idle session is removed and resets when traffic passes. duration counts up from the start, and timeout is the configured TTL."),
("What does the Hit Count column on a firewall policy show?",
 ["Admins who edited it", "Bytes it has blocked", "Times it was moved", "Sessions it matched"], "D",
 "The hit count rises each time a new session matches the policy, which helps find unused or shadowed policies. It can be cleared to start a fresh count."),
("A firewall service object matches traffic on what?",
 ["Protocol and port", "Application signature", "URL category", "Source MAC address"], "A",
 "Services match on IP protocol and port numbers. Recognising an application whatever port it uses is the job of application control."),
("A policy allows sessions from port1 to port2. What is needed for the replies from port2?",
 ["A matching reverse policy", "Nothing extra", "A local-in policy", "A virtual IP"], "B",
 "FortiGate is stateful, so replies that belong to an accepted session pass on that session. A reverse policy is only needed for new sessions started from port2."),
("A policy must match every address except one address group. What does the administrator set?",
 ["A second address group", "A geography address", "Negate the source", "An exclusion zone"], "C",
 "Negating the source makes the policy match every address except those listed. A geography address only matches by country."),
("In profile-based NGFW mode, where is flow-based or proxy-based inspection chosen?",
 ["Per VDOM only", "Per interface", "Per security profile", "Per firewall policy"], "D",
 "Each firewall policy sets its own inspection mode, flow-based or proxy-based, and the profiles attached must support that mode."),
]
assert len(ITEMS) == 24
for i, it in enumerate(ITEMS):
    assert it[2] == "ABCD"[i % 4], i
    assert len(set(it[1])) == 4, i
